Scoring depends heavily on visual similarity. Short symbolic equations (Dj, beta01) look nearly identical — visual can't tell them apart.

Anchors are all-or-nothing. No confidence tiers. A single false anchor cuts the document in the wrong place.

Segments are hard boundaries. If the correct PDF partner is in a different segment, it can never be matched.

DP is order-preserving. If the reference alignment isn't monotone (599, 12483, 8948 all have inversions), the DP is structurally capped — no score can fix it.

One wrong match cascades. Because the DP preserves order, a single error locks everything after it in the segment.

~25 refine passes are hardcoded patches. Each fixes one specific failure mode. They interact unpredictably. Some help, some misfire, some shift blocks by one position (599's main bug).

Refine passes are file-specific in places. Some are gated by if pair_id == "...". These overfit and can't generalize.

PDF bboxes are sometimes wrong. Crops capture only part of the equation, so visual score compares a fragment against a full expression.

No cross-segment recovery. If the DP leaves a docx item unmatched, nothing tries the neighbor segments.

Position and page penalties are fixed constants. Same values for all files, regardless of layout.

No signal when mapping is uncertain. It commits to a pair silently. UAT sees a wrong match, not "unsure."
